amount None -> other-income! Wait Rule.matches `transaction.amount or 0`, so built-in salary description matches and returns salary despite None. Category salary. Corrected transaction salary. Fingerprints differ. Import adds correct one. Journal contains old null and new 2750. Report conversion iterates old first, crashes. So for user currently affected, patch alone does not make their existing ledger report! This is significant. They explicitly say \"Since I imported my March statement... crashes\" and asks fix so importing and reporting gives right totals. If they update code and re-import, old corrupt transaction remains. A proper fix should address poisoned ledger, perhaps migration.\n\nBut maybe expected usage assumes delete ledger? No, ledgerly is real personal ledger; fix should not require manual JSON editing. README import promise retroactively? They may need a repair path. We should consider adding robust repair of null-amount transactions when re-importing source, or automatic cleanup.\n\nOption A: Change JSON serialization to no longer allow None; but old ledger remains.\nOption B: When loading, reject null with clear error (doesn't satisfy reporting).\nOption C: On import, replace existing null transaction with corrected one based on source.\nOption D: On report, ignore null transaction (drops salary because old null, but corrected reimport adds salary; ignoring old avoids crash). But \"never drop money\" and null is corrupt, ignoring questionable.\nOption E: On load, automatically drop null records (and warning). This drops known-corrupt placeholder, but no actual amount represented; then reimport adds real salary. Could work but silently modifying user's ledger and no warning, violates no drop/invent.\nOption F: During import, detect and replace null entry with same source. Most direct. Could also perhaps error instructing delete? But user asks fix.\n\nImplement Journal replacement by source? Need carefully preserve semantics and atomicity. Could add