e. Fine.\nWhat if old invalid entry source same but different category, replaced correct.\nGood.\n\nBut Transaction model says amount Decimal, but runtime none. `Journal.add` can detect.\n\nAdd test simulating poisoned ledger:\n```\nbook = journal()\nbook.add(Transaction(... amount=None,... source=\"march.csv:5\")) # type ignore\nresult = import_statement(fixed)\nassert result.added=3? Wait journal has old invalid salary source march.csv:5. Import categorised has rent/groceries/salary. extend loops rent add, grocery add, salary detects invalid and replaces, returns True. added=3. len(book)=3, no null.\nreport works.\n```\nCould use `book.add(txn(\"?\"))` can't None. Create `Transaction` with type ignore. Existing test style allows ignores. Or round-trip `Transaction.from_dict` null.\nThis would be a valuable regression and ensure actual user recovery.\n\nBut is this within reference expected? It changes Journal.add semantics to repair. User says \"Everything that works today keep working same\"; only invalid data scenario changes. Great.\n\nPotential atomic import with invalid statement: We do not call extend until all parsed. Fine.\nPotential `Journal.add` replacement transaction fingerprint and `_seen`: Must remove old fingerprint from set. Since old fingerprint may equal new? no. If old amount None and new amount string different. Could be new amount? Decimal can't None. Remove then add.\nCode:\n```\ndef add(self, transaction):\n    ...\n    for index, existing in enumerate(self._entries):\n       if (\n         existing.source == transaction.source\n         and existing.account == transaction.account\n         and existing.amount is None\n       ):\n          self._entries[index] = transaction\n          self._seen.remove(existing.fingerprint)\n          self._seen.add(transaction.fingerprint)\n          return True\n    if transaction.fingerprint in self._seen...\n```\nValidation order: check account/category before replacement. Good.\nShould check `source` nonempty to avoid accidental replacement of two no-source null transactions:\n```\nand transaction.source\n```\nAdd. Old imported records always source. Good.\nBut `existing.amount is None` type issue. We can use:\n```\nand existing.to_dict()[\"amount\"] is None\n```\nSilly. `getattr(existing, \"amount\") is None`. Python. Existing transactions.py uses `None if self.amount is None`, so style permits. Use direct.\n\nWould `Journal.add` be called to load old ledger and accept null; yes. Then fix import replacement. If report before reimport still TypeError. Could make report skip invalid? Better not; tell user reimport required. But user wants after importing. They likely will re-import after code update. We can perhaps CLI import automatically. Great.\n\nShould we include this extra behavior? The user asks fix so \"importing this statement and reporting March gives right totals\"; if their current ledger already contains bad entry, after patch they run import again, it gets repaired. This is ideal.\n\nLet's inspect transaction fingerprint comments and identity. No need.\n\nHowever, imported old null salary categorization: `Categorizer.matches` handles `transaction.amount or 0` and salary rule returns salary despite None because description matches; yes. Reimport replacement works.\n\nWhat if old bad transaction source is `march.csv:5` while new statement sample salary line is line 5; yes. If they shortened statement vs actual statement line number differs, source might differ (actual salary line maybe line 25), old and new same file/line if same full file. If they use shortened sample file different line, old source mismatch and crash persists. But actual same statement file likely.\n\nCould instead identify corrupted transaction by account/date/description/counterparty, but source is exact best. No.\n\nDo we need expose user instruction to re-import? final can say \"Re-import March to replace the previously null amount.\" This is helpful. If we implement repair.\n\nDo