`\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n...\nclass Journal:\n  _entries\n  _seen\n```\nCould build missing maps in extend:\n```\ndef extend(self, transactions, *, repair_missing=False):\n    items=list(transactions)\n    for t in items: self._validate(t)\n    if not repair_missing:\n      return self._extend_items(items)\n    missing_by_source = defaultdict(list)\n    missing_by_semantic = defaultdict(list)\n    for index,t in enumerate(self._entries):\n      if t.amount is None:\n        missing_by_source[(t.account,t.source)].append(index)\n        missing_by_semantic[self._missing_key(t)].append(index)\n    added=dups=0\n    for t in items:\n      index = self._take_missing(t,...)\n      if index is not None:\n         existing=self._entries[index]\n         if t.fingerprint in self._seen:\n            self._entries.pop(index)\n            self._seen.discard(existing.fingerprint)\n            duplicates +=1\n         else:\n            self._entries[index]=t\n            self._seen.discard(existing.fingerprint)\n            self._seen.add(t.fingerprint)\n            added +=1\n      else:\n         if self.add(t): added++ else duplicates++\n    return\n```\nIssue indices in lists can become stale if entries popped/replaced? We never pop except candidate duplicate case, which removes one missing entry, shifting indices of later entries. Maps contain indices -> stale. Use transaction object references instead of indices. `_entries` list entries are immutable dataclasses. Map lists of Transaction objects. On replacement find identity? `self._entries.index(existing)` O(n) per repair; number repairs usually small. Could maintain mapping but complexity. Or remove missing from `_entries` via list comprehension each repair O(n), still not O(n²) unless many repairs. Could build a set of missing transactions and at end apply replacements/removals atomically in one pass.\n\nPlan phase:\n- Build `missing_by_source`/semantic mapping to Transaction object.\n- For each candidat