{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794915438180,"sessionID":"ses_e16be6397ffeTX69h5hAKoGVW8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"32293f1a-5ae7-9719-9107-fe967ca492c8_0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Re-review import fix","prompt":"Re-review the updated uncommitted diff after the fixes. Verify whether your prior findings 1-6 are resolved, especially renamed-file recovery, de-duplication, load-path behavior, performance, atomic failed imports, and balance/schema behavior. Do not edit files. Run relevant tests and report only remaining concrete correctness issues relevant to the user request, ranked by severity, with file/line references.","subagent_type":"general","task_id":"ses_e16ba48dfffeiPnmEC22Xcks6J"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_e16ba48dfffeiPnmEC22Xcks6J\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nRe-reviewed the updated diff (`ledgerly/core/journal.py`, `ledgerly/io/bank_formats.py`, `ledgerly/io/csv_import.py`, `tests/test_import.py`). All 40 tests pass; `ruff check` clean.\n\n## Prior findings 1–6: status\n\n| # | Finding | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Renamed-file recovery | **Resolved** — matching now falls back to transaction details, not the file name (`journal.py:84-85`). Re-importing `march.csv` as `March-2026.csv` now repairs the broken row and `report income` works. |\n| 2 | Dedup bypass (double-counted row) | **Resolved** — the fingerprint check now runs before the replacement (`journal.py:94-101`); a redundant broken row is *deleted* instead of duplicated. Verified: 1 entry, groceries 12.50 (was 25.00). |\n| 3 | Load-path data loss | **Resolved** — repair only runs under `repair_missing=True` (`journal.py:117-126`), which only `import_statement` sets (`csv_import.py:92`). Loading a ledger with two same-source rows now keeps both. |\n| 4 | O(n²) `add()` | **Resolved** — 20 000 rows: 26.2 s → **0.13 s**. |\n| 5 | Failed import mutated the journal | **Resolved** — all rows are validated before any mutation (`journal.py:122-124`). A failed import now leaves the journal untouched. |\n| 6 | Balance/schema behaviour | **Partly resolved** — the \"missing column 'Bedrag'\" error for saldo rows is restored (`bank_formats.py:114` now reads the column before the balance check). The other half stands as a deliberate, now-tested change (see Low 3). |\n\n## Remaining issues\n\n**High — a repair that only *removes* a broken row is never written to disk.**\n`journal.py:94-97` deletes the broken row but counts it as a *duplicate* (`duplicates += 1`), leaving `added == 0`. The CLI decides whether to save on `result.added > 0` (`__main__.py:106`), so the ledger file is never rewritten and the null-amount row stays on disk:\n\n```\n=== import (the broken row can only be dropped, nothing new to add) ===\nrc: 0 | stdout: april.csv: 1 read, 0 added, 1 already there\n--- the ledger file now holds ---\n    old-march-copy.csv:5 None     Salaris maart   <-- still there\n    march.csv:5         2750.00   Salaris maart\n=== report income afterwards ===\nrc: 1 | stderr tail: TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for *: 'NoneType' and 'decimal.Decimal'\n```\n\nIn memory the row *is* dropped (verified separately), so this is purely the persistence decision. Fix: count repairs/removals in `ImportResult` (e.g. a `repaired` field) and include them in the CLI's `changed` check, or count the removal as `added`.\n\n**Medium — an unmatched null-amount row still kills every report with a traceback.**\nThe import side is fixed, but the read side is not: `convert.py:16` and `export.py:28` still do arithmetic on `None`, and `__main__.py:156` only catches `LedgerError`, so `report income`/`export` exit 1 with a raw traceback instead of `error: …` / exit 2. The repair only fires when account+date+currency+description+counterparty+iban all match (`journal.py:46-55`); if the counterparty text differs between exports, or the statement is never re-imported, the row stays and the ledger is unusable with an unhandled exception.\n\n**Medium — the details fallback can attach the wrong amount.**\n`journal.py:84-85` matches on details that exclude the amount and the line, so a broken row is repaired by the *first* incoming transaction with the same details. With two same-day, same-description, same-counterparty payments of different amounts, the broken row takes the other one's amount (totals stay correct, per-line attribution swaps); if the matching transaction is from a different statement line than the broken one, the recorded amount is simply wrong. Matching on `(account, booked, description, counterparty, iban)` plus the *nearest* amount would be safer.\n\n**Low — new mypy errors in a previously clean file.**\n`journal.py:81` (`existing = None` after `existing` was inferred as `Transaction`) and `journal.py:83` (`Transaction | None` assigned to `Transaction`). Baseline `HEAD` had 4 errors in 4 files; the patch makes it 6 in 5. `existing: Transaction | None = None` fixes both. Under `--warn-unreachable`, lines 85 and 87 are also reported as dead.\n\n**Low — the import summary hides repairs.**\n`ImportResult` has no counter for replaced/removed rows, so the CLI prints \"0 added, 1 already there\" for an import that actually deleted a broken row (same root cause as the High finding).\n\n**Low — the mutation loop is still quadratic in the number of repairs.**\n`journal.py:105` scans `_entries` linearly per repair (`next(i for i, entry in enumerate(...) if entry is existing)`): 5 000 broken rows repaired in 0.32 s, but it grows as O(n·r) (≈5 s at 20 000). An id→index map would keep it linear.\n\n**Low — one deliberate behaviour change remains.**\nA saldo row that *carries* an amount is no longer imported as money (`bank_formats.py:114-116`); `HEAD` imported it (e.g. `1.000,00` \"Saldo begin\"). This is now covered by `tests/test_import.py:68` (`test_rabo_does_not_import_a_balance_as_money`), but it is still a change to existing behaviour for statements whose saldo rows carry amounts — worth a conscious sign-off given \"existing behavior must remain unchanged\".\n\n## Verdict\nThe four requirement-level goals hold: `+` amounts import, unreadable lines raise `file:line` and add nothing, March income includes the salary (2,750.00 EUR), and 40/40 tests pass. The one blocking issue is the persistence of removal-only repairs: without it, the repair can report success while the broken row — and the resulting report crash — remains in `ledger.json`.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_e16be6397ffeTX69h5hAKoGVW8","sessionId":"ses_e16ba48dfffeiPnmEC22Xcks6J","model":{"modelID":"minimax/minimax-m3.1-flash","providerID":"openrouter"},"truncated":false},"title":"Re-review import fix","time":{"start":1794915330099,"end":1794915438173}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Need maybe add atomic failed import test. The malformed unreadable test covers requirement. We altered extend to atomic all validation, but no test. Could test if we claim. Not necessary but good. Yet reviewer might review.\n\nLet's ask same reviewer agent resume to re-review fixes. Use task_id.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e94a682c001sW5kCnjfzSegqN","sessionID":"ses_e16be6397ffeTX69h5hAKoGVW8","messageID":"msg_1e94a62d7001EkCDDrlgh3mZsg"}}
