{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794915609919,"sessionID":"ses_e16be6397ffeTX69h5hAKoGVW8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"000d3c8e-629c-9bf9-800c-8ce6475ca0cb_0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Final import fix review","prompt":"Perform one final review of the current diff after the latest persistence, repair-count, atomicity, and actionable-error changes. Confirm the prior blocking persistence issue is fixed and check for any new concrete correctness regressions in the requested import/report flow. Do not edit. Run tests and report only actionable findings relevant to the user request, with severity and file/line references.","subagent_type":"general","task_id":"ses_e16ba48dfffeiPnmEC22Xcks6J"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_e16ba48dfffeiPnmEC22Xcks6J\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nFinal review of the current diff. 42/42 tests pass; `mypy` is back to the pre-patch baseline (4 errors, all in untouched files); `ruff` reports only the pre-existing E741 in `tests/test_budget_export.py`.\n\n## Prior blocking issue: fixed\n\nVerified end-to-end, not just by the new test:\n\n- **Persistence of a removal-only repair** — `__main__.py:106` now uses `result.added > 0 or result.repaired > 0`. A repair that only drops a redundant broken row is written to disk:\n  ```\n  april.csv: 1 read, 0 added, 1 already there, 1 repaired\n  --- the ledger file now holds ---   march.csv:5 2750.00 Salaris maart\n  === report income afterwards ===    rc: 0\n  ```\n  Previously the broken row stayed in `ledger.json` and the report crashed.\n- **Actionable error instead of a traceback** — `transactions.py:51-56` + `fx/convert.py:20`, `io/export.py:28`, `reports/balance.py:31`:\n  ```\n  report income -> rc 2 | error: transaction from march.csv:5 has no amount; re-import that statement\n  export        -> rc 2 | error: transaction from march.csv:5 has no amount; re-import that statement\n  ```\n  (was an unhandled `TypeError`, rc 1, with a traceback).\n- **Full user flow** (fresh ledger → import → report → re-import → broken statement → old broken ledger → repair):\n  ```\n  1) march.csv: 3 read, 3 added          5) error: transaction from march.csv:5 has no amount; …\n  2) report income -> 2,750.00 EUR       6) march.csv: 3 read, 0 added, 2 already there, 1 repaired\n  3) re-import -> 0 added, 3 already     7) Total income 2,750.00 / Total expenses -1,084.35 / Net 1,665.65\n  4) bad.csv:5: cannot read the amount '2.750'  (ledger untouched)\n  ```\n- Still green from earlier rounds: renamed-file repair (matched by details, `journal.py:84-85`), no duplicate fingerprint, load path keeps both rows, failed import leaves the journal untouched, 20 000 rows import in 0.13 s, and the repair path is now linear (5 000 broken rows: 0.32 s → 0.057 s) thanks to the `id()`-based rebuild (`journal.py:104-118`).\n\n## Remaining findings (no blockers)\n\n**Low — the details fallback can attach a different statement's amount.** `journal.py:84-85` matches on `_missing_key` (`journal.py:46-55`: account, date, currency, description, counterparty, iban) with no amount, so a broken row can be \"repaired\" with a transaction that merely looks similar:\n\n```\njournal: broken row (amount None, \"Salaris maart\", Acme BV, 2026-03-25)\nimport:  march-renamed.csv -> \"+3.000,00\"  (same details, different amount)\nresult:  march-renamed.csv: 1 read, 0 added, 0 already there, 1 repaired\n         march-renamed.csv:2 3000.00 Salaris maart      <-- the broken row's amount is a guess\n```\nThe source key is tried first (`journal.py:83`), so this only bites when the statement was renamed *and* the amount changed. Any amount assigned to an amount-less row is inherently a guess, but when several candidates match the same details the repair should either pick the closest/only candidate or refuse and report, instead of silently taking the first.\n\n**Low — `Transaction.is_income` is the one public accessor still unguarded.** `transactions.py:47-49` does `self.amount > 0`, which raises a bare `TypeError` (not the actionable `LedgerError`) on an amount-less row. It is currently unused, but `return self.require_amount() > 0` costs nothing and keeps the class consistent.\n\n**Low — the declared type still lies.** `Transaction.amount` is `Decimal` (`transactions.py:19`) while `from_dict` can produce `None` (`transactions.py:71-74`), which is why `require_amount` needs a `cast` (`transactions.py:52`) and the repair loop needs `getattr(entry, \"amount\", None)` (`journal.py:61`). Declaring `amount: Decimal | None` would let the type checker enforce the guards now spread over five modules.\n\n**Info — one accepted behaviour change still needs sign-off.** A saldo row that carries an amount is no longer imported as money (`bank_formats.py:114-116`; `HEAD` imported it as e.g. `1.000,00`). It is now deliberate and covered by `tests/test_import.py:84-88`, but it does deviate from \"existing behavior must remain unchanged\" for statements whose saldo rows carry amounts. The \"missing column 'Bedrag'\" error for saldo rows was correctly restored.\n\n## Verdict\nThe blocking persistence defect is fixed, the report path now fails with an actionable message instead of a traceback, and the requested flow (signed `+` amounts import, unreadable lines error with file/line and add nothing, March income includes the salary at 2,750.00 EUR, existing behaviour otherwise unchanged, tests pass) is met. The remaining items are hardening nits, not blockers.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_e16be6397ffeTX69h5hAKoGVW8","sessionId":"ses_e16ba48dfffeiPnmEC22Xcks6J","model":{"modelID":"minimax/minimax-m3.1-flash","providerID":"openrouter"},"truncated":false},"title":"Final import fix review","time":{"start":1794915512013,"end":1794915609904}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Mypy only preexisting errors (line transactions shifted but existing). Good. Need maybe no new errors. Reviewer said baseline 4 in 4 files; now 3? fine.\n\nNeed ask reviewer one more time focused remaining high/medium.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e94d2ec8001eds207ge5xolRG","sessionID":"ses_e16be6397ffeTX69h5hAKoGVW8","messageID":"msg_1e94d2782001JLB9C8AHfLrhnG"}}
